DO NOT include work that is related to maintenance, big fixes on existing capabilities, or non-Research and Development work.
</qualified_r_and_d_project_definition>

For each project in your analysis, wrap your analysis in <project_analysis> tags. Consider and explicitly address the following points:
- List the key features of the project that align with the definition of a qualified R&D project.
- For each criterion in the R&D definition, explain how the project meets it. Provide specific examples from the project description.
- Compare the project with already distilled projects, listing similarities and differences.
- Is it similar to other projects that can be combined? If so, which ones and why? Consider the goals, implemented work, experimentation, and outcomes.
- Is it a duplicate of an already distilled project? If so, which one?
- If combining projects, justify why they should be merged into a single high-level project.
- If keeping a project separate, explain why it cannot be combined with others.

It's okay for this section to be quite long, as thorough evaluation is crucial for accurate project extraction.

After your analysis, provide a list of extracted R&D projects.
Each project in your output MUST include:
- name: A descriptive name for the project related to what the project hopes to accomplish.
- description: A summary of the project. The description can be quite long, is based ONLY on the provided content, is very detailed and verbose, contains ALL of the information related to the project, and must include the following sections:
    - Overview: A descriptive and detailed overview of the project.
    - Goals: The specific and detailed goals of the project.
    - Technological Challenges: Specific and detailed technological challenges that the project aims to overcome. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
    - Ideas: Specific and detailed outline of any innovative ideas or approaches discussed in the content related to the project. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
    - Experimentation: Specific details on the experimentation or analysis that was actually conducted as part of the project. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
    - Implemented work: Specific and detailed outline of artifacts of work that were actually implemented and executed as part of the project.
                        This could be work that was done, experimentation, or analysis that was conducted. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
    - Outcomes: Specific and detailed outcomes or results of the project. These can include new technical capabilities, knowledge, products, or processes. Label the outcomes with these categories. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
    - Other Information: Any additional specific and detailed information about the project that is important or interesting. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
    - Project Qualification: Specific and detailed reasoning for why the project qualifies as an R&D project, according to the provided definition. Give examples from the provided content when possible.
- source_content_ids: The source_content_ids of the content that the project came from.

If your project is created from multiple source_content_ids, you MUST group ALL source_content_ids together into a list.

Remember:
- DO NOT duplicate projects that have already been distilled into high-level projects.
- Ensure that your final output only includes new or newly combined projects that meet the definition of a qualified R&D project.
- Be thorough in your analysis but concise in your final output.
